<section class="notes"><h2>What this first pass tells us</h2><p><strong>A has the closest palette match.</strong> Its identity comes from the champion’s silhouette and technique. B adds a focal accent without restoring full-color clothing or scenery. The cool accents on warm cards and gold accents on cool cards are alternatives to test, rather than a new gameplay color code.</p><p><strong>The brushwork still needs a final consistency pass.</strong> Imdugud and Huwawa retain stronger outlines than the main cards. Huwawa’s A draft also has one stray gold stroke; strict monochrome would remove it. The generated A/B compositions are close, but have minor drawing differences.</p><p><strong>Applied direction:</strong> A is selected for all 24 champion techniques. The production pass removes the study’s stray accent and keeps the scenery subordinate to the move. B remains here as an archived alternative.</p><h2>Full study sheets &amp; generation notes</h2><div class="sheet-links"><a href="adventure/assets/palette-study/warm-v1.webp">Red / orange ↗</a><a href="adventure/assets/palette-study/earth-v1.webp">Yellow / green ↗</a><a href="adventure/assets/palette-study/celestial-v1.webp">Blue / purple ↗</a><a href="adventure/assets/palette-study/prompts.json">Exact prompts &amp; references ↗</a></div><p>Generated with the built-in imagegen tool from the existing champion illustrations and main-card style references. Top row: A. Bottom row: B.</p></section>
